          id: '103_2_8_4',
          question: 'Analyze the significance of Hebrew monotheism in the history of world religion.',
          answer: 'Hebrew monotheism—the belief in one universal God (YHWH) revealed through covenant with the Israelite people—was one of the most consequential religious innovations in human history, forming the foundation for Judaism, Christianity, and Islam and shaping the moral and theological thought of more than half of humanity.\n\nThe development of Hebrew monotheism was gradual. Early Israelite religion was likely henotheistic—worshipping one god (YHWH) while acknowledging the existence of others. The Hebrew Bible records a progressive movement toward strict monotheism (the belief that only one God exists): the period of the Exodus (c. 13th century BCE), the monarchy (c. 1000 BCE), and particularly the period of the great prophets (8th–6th centuries BCE, including Isaiah, Jeremiah, and Ezekiel) are associated with increasingly radical monotheism.\n\nThe theological distinctiveness of Hebrew monotheism compared to polytheistic neighbors was significant. (1) Universal God: YHWH was not merely the tribal god of Israel but the creator of the universe, the God of all peoples. (2) Ethical monotheism: God demanded ethical behavior from humans—justice, mercy, truth—not merely ritual sacrifice. The prophets (especially Amos and Micah) insisted that God cares more about social justice than temple worship. (3) History as sacred: God acts in history—the Exodus, the return from exile—giving linear historical time moral significance. (4) Written scripture: The Torah (Five Books of Moses) became the canonical text of Jewish practice and theology.\n\nHebraic monotheism profoundly influenced Christianity (which universalized it) and Islam (which accepted Hebrew prophets into its own tradition), making the Hebrew theological innovation the most globally influential religious development in world history.',
