          id: '103_3_8_6',
          question: 'Describe the Athenian democracy and its significance for political thought.',
          answer: 'Athenian democracy (c. 508–322 BCE), the world\'s first direct democracy, was a radical political experiment that, despite its significant limitations, introduced fundamental concepts—popular sovereignty, equality before the law, citizen participation in government—that have profoundly shaped subsequent Western political thought and modern democratic institutions.\n\nAthenian democracy was established by Cleisthenes (508 BCE), who reorganized the citizen body into demes (local units) and tribes cutting across old aristocratic clan divisions. The central institutions of Athenian democracy were: the Ekklesia (Assembly)—open to all adult male citizens, who voted directly on legislation and foreign policy; the Boule (Council of 500)—a body of 500 citizens chosen by lot to set the Assembly\'s agenda and execute decisions; and the law courts—large juries of citizens (typically 201–501 members) also chosen by lot who decided cases.\n\nPericles (c. 495–429 BCE), the dominant Athenian statesman, extended democratic participation—introducing pay for jury service (making participation available to poorer citizens) and celebrating Athenian democracy in his famous Funeral Oration (recorded by Thucydides): "Our constitution is called a democracy because power is in the hands not of a minority but of the whole people."\n\nAthenian democracy had significant limitations: women, slaves (who constituted a large portion of the population), and metics (resident foreigners) were excluded from citizenship and political participation. Slavery was fundamental to the Athenian economy. The democracy also made tragic decisions—condemning Socrates to death, launching the disastrous Sicilian Expedition.\n\nDespite these limitations, Athenian democracy introduced the foundational idea—revolutionary in its time—that political authority derives from the consent of the governed, not from divine right or aristocratic birth.',
