          answer: 'Confucianism, the philosophical system derived from the teachings of Confucius (Kong Qiu, 551–479 BCE), was the dominant intellectual and ethical tradition of Chinese civilization for over 2,000 years—shaping governance, social organization, education, and family life across China, Korea, Japan, Vietnam, and other East Asian societies.\n\nConfucius lived during the chaotic Warring States period and sought to restore social order through moral cultivation. His teachings, recorded in the Analects by his disciples, centered on five key relationships: ruler-subject, father-son, husband-wife, elder brother-younger brother, and friend-friend. Each relationship involves reciprocal obligations: superiors owe benevolence and protection; inferiors owe loyalty and respect. The ideal of ren (benevolence/humaneness) was the supreme virtue—the quality of being truly human in one\'s relationships.\n\nThe five Confucian classics—the I Ching, Book of History, Book of Odes, Spring and Autumn Annals, and Book of Rites—became the curriculum of Chinese education. The Imperial Examination System (established 605 CE, abolished 1905 CE), based on mastery of Confucian texts, was the pathway to government service—creating a meritocratic bureaucracy selected by knowledge rather than birth. This system profoundly shaped Chinese governance for 1,300 years.\n\nConfucian social ethics emphasized: filial piety (respect for parents and ancestors) as the root of all virtue; social harmony through proper role fulfillment; moral self-cultivation as the basis of good governance; and the importance of ritual (li) as the medium through which proper relationships are enacted.\n\nConfucian values—respect for education, family cohesion, social hierarchy, and civil service—are widely credited for the historical success of East Asian economies and remain influential in contemporary China, Japan, Korea, and Singapore.',
