          answer: 'The Byzantine Empire (330–1453 CE), the eastern continuation of the Roman Empire, preserved classical Greek and Roman culture through the medieval period and played a pivotal role in transmitting ancient knowledge to the Renaissance West and in shaping Eastern Orthodox Christian civilization across Eastern Europe and Russia.\n\nFounded when Emperor Constantine I moved the Roman capital to Constantinople (modern Istanbul) in 330 CE, the Byzantine Empire lasted over a thousand years after the fall of the western Roman Empire (476 CE). At its height under Justinian I (r. 527–565 CE), it reconquered North Africa, Italy, and southern Spain, temporarily reuniting much of the Roman world. Justinian\'s most enduring achievement was the Corpus Juris Civilis—the systematic codification of Roman law that became the foundation of modern civil law.\n\nByzantine culture was a synthesis of Greek, Roman, and Christian traditions. Greek became the language of administration and culture. Byzantine art—characterized by golden mosaics, flat stylized figures with large eyes, and a focus on spiritual rather than naturalistic representation—developed a distinctive aesthetic that influenced Orthodox Christian art across Eastern Europe. The Hagia Sophia (completed 537 CE), Justinian\'s great church in Constantinople, was the largest domed building in the world for a thousand years—a masterpiece of architectural engineering.\n\nByzantium served as a cultural bridge: preserving Greek philosophical and scientific texts during the Western European "Dark Ages," transmitting them to the Arab world (where they were translated and elaborated), and eventually returning them to Western Europe during the Renaissance. When Constantinople fell to the Ottoman Turks in 1453 CE, Greek scholars fleeing to Italy brought manuscripts that directly contributed to the Renaissance.',
