          question: 'Describe the feudal system in medieval Europe and its characteristics.',
          answer: 'Feudalism was the dominant social, political, and economic system of medieval Western Europe (c. 9th–15th centuries CE), characterized by a hierarchical arrangement of land tenure and military obligation, in which land (in the form of a "fief") was granted by a lord to a vassal in exchange for military service and loyalty.\n\nThe feudal pyramid had the king at the top, who theoretically owned all land. Kings granted large estates to nobles (barons, counts, dukes) who became royal vassals, pledging fealty (loyalty) and military service. These great lords subdivided their land to lesser lords (knights) who became their vassals. At the bottom were the serfs—peasants legally bound to the land who farmed it in exchange for military protection from their lord.\n\nThe manor was the basic economic unit of feudalism. The lord\'s manor comprised: the demesne (land farmed directly by the lord using serf labor); peasant strips in open fields; common lands for grazing; and the lord\'s residence (manor house or castle). Serfs paid dues to the lord in the form of labor (working the demesne), produce, and fees for using the lord\'s mill, oven, and wine press.\n\nThe Church was integrated into the feudal system: bishops and abbots held land as feudal lords, owed military service to the king, and administered significant territories. The concept of "benefit of clergy" (clerics tried by Church rather than royal courts) created tensions between papal and royal authority throughout the medieval period.\n\nFeudalism declined from the 11th century onward due to: the growth of towns and a money economy (which allowed lords to hire soldiers instead of depending on vassals); the Black Death (which gave surviving peasants bargaining power); the growth of royal power (which centralized authority away from feudal lords); and the development of paid professional armies.',
