          answer: 'The Industrial Revolution (c. 1760–1850), beginning in Britain and spreading to Europe and North America, was one of the most transformative events in human history—replacing human and animal power with machine power, shifting production from rural homes to urban factories, and creating the modern capitalist industrial economy.\n\nCauses of the Industrial Revolution in Britain: (1) Agricultural Revolution: enclosure of common lands increased agricultural productivity and released rural labor for industrial employment. (2) Natural resources: Britain had large coal and iron deposits close to navigable waterways and ports. (3) Capital and banking: British merchants and financiers had capital to invest in new industries; the Bank of England (1694) and capital markets provided investment channels. (4) Empire and trade: British colonial markets provided raw materials and consumers for manufactured goods. (5) Political stability: Britain\'s constitutional monarchy and property rights protection encouraged investment. (6) Key inventions: James Watt\'s steam engine (1769), the spinning jenny, and the power loom mechanized production.\n\nConsequences: (1) Urbanization: populations shifted from rural areas to industrial cities. Manchester grew from 25,000 to 300,000 inhabitants between 1772 and 1850. Living conditions in early industrial cities were appalling—overcrowding, pollution, child labor, unsafe working conditions. (2) Class transformation: the Industrial Revolution created the industrial bourgeoisie (factory owners, capitalists) and the proletariat (wage laborers)—the class conflict between these groups is central to Karl Marx\'s analysis and inspired the labor movement. (3) Global economic dominance: British manufactured goods (textiles, machinery, steel) were exported worldwide; by 1850, Britain was the "Workshop of the World," producing half the world\'s iron and cotton cloth. (4) Technological acceleration: the factory system created systematic technological innovation, institutionalizing invention.',
