          id: '103_5_8_3',
          question: 'Describe the French Revolution and its impact on European politics.',
          answer: 'The French Revolution (1789–1799) was one of the most transformative political events in modern history—abolishing the French monarchy and aristocracy, proclaiming the principles of Liberty, Equality, and Fraternity, and eventually inspiring democratic and nationalist movements across Europe and the world.\n\nCauses: (1) Financial crisis: France was bankrupt from wars (including supporting the American Revolution); King Louis XVI convened the Estates-General (a consultative body not meeting since 1614) to raise taxes. (2) Social inequalities: the First Estate (clergy) and Second Estate (nobility) paid no taxes; the Third Estate (98% of the population) bore the tax burden. (3) Enlightenment ideas: Rousseau\'s concept of popular sovereignty, Voltaire\'s attacks on tyranny, and Montesquieu\'s separation of powers had created an intellectual climate hostile to absolute monarchy. (4) Food shortages: poor harvests in 1788 created bread shortages for urban workers.\n\nKey events: The Storming of the Bastille (July 14, 1789) symbolically launched the Revolution. The Declaration of the Rights of Man and Citizen (August 1789) proclaimed individual liberty, legal equality, and popular sovereignty. The execution of Louis XVI (1793) radicalized the Revolution. The Terror (1793–1794) under Robespierre and the Committee of Public Safety executed approximately 17,000 people and imprisoned 250,000 more. Napoleon Bonaparte seized power in 1799, ending the Revolution phase.\n\nConsequences: (1) Abolition of feudalism and aristocratic privilege—France became a meritocratic society. (2) Spread of revolutionary ideas across Europe—inspiring the 1848 revolutions. (3) Codification of law in the Napoleonic Code (1804)—spreading principles of legal equality and civil rights. (4) Nationalism: the French Revolution created modern nationalism—the idea that the sovereign state belongs to "the nation" (the people) rather than the king.',
