          id: '103_5_8_5',
          question: 'Examine the Protestant Reformation and its significance for European civilization.',
          answer: 'The Protestant Reformation (beginning 1517) was a religious, social, and political revolution that shattered the religious unity of Western Christendom, directly challenged medieval structures of authority, and contributed to the emergence of modernity.\n\nMartin Luther (1483–1546), an Augustinian monk and theology professor, launched the Reformation with his Ninety-Five Theses (October 31, 1517)—a scholarly challenge to the sale of indulgences (papal pardons sold for money). His deeper theological challenge was twofold: salvation by faith alone (sola fide), not by works or Church sacraments; and scripture alone (sola scriptura) as the authority for Christian belief, not papal tradition or decree. These principles directly challenged papal authority. Excommunicated in 1521, Luther translated the Bible into German (standardizing the German language) and organized the Lutheran Church.\n\nJohn Calvin (1509–1564) developed Calvinism—centered on predestination and strict moral discipline—which spread to France (Huguenots), Scotland (Presbyterians), the Netherlands, and England (Puritans). The English Reformation (Henry VIII breaking with Rome in 1534) was initially political rather than theological but eventually produced Anglicanism.\n\nConsequences: (1) Religious wars—the Thirty Years\' War (1618–1648) devastated Germany; the Peace of Westphalia (1648) established religious tolerance and the modern nation-state system. (2) Literacy—Protestant insistence on Bible reading promoted universal education. (3) Individualism—the Reformation established individual conscience as a moral authority, contributing to modern individualism and eventually human rights. (4) Capitalism—Max Weber\'s thesis connects Calvinist work ethic to the spirit of capitalism. (5) The Counter-Reformation strengthened the Catholic Church through the Council of Trent, the Jesuits, and the Inquisition, producing its own cultural renaissance in Baroque art and architecture.',
