          id: '103_5_8_6',
          question: 'Describe the Scientific Revolution and its significance for the development of modern science.',
          answer: 'The Scientific Revolution (c. 1543–1687) was the period in which the foundations of modern science were laid—replacing the medieval synthesis of Aristotle and Christian theology with new empirical methods and mathematical theories that transformed human understanding of the cosmos, physics, anatomy, and chemistry.\n\nNicolas Copernicus (1473–1543) launched the revolution with his heliocentric model of the solar system (On the Revolutions of the Celestial Spheres, 1543)—proposing that the earth revolves around the sun, not vice versa. This directly contradicted Aristotelian cosmology and Church teaching. Tycho Brahe\'s systematic astronomical observations provided the empirical data; Johannes Kepler (1571–1630) used Brahe\'s data to formulate his three laws of planetary motion—proving that planets move in ellipses, not perfect circles.\n\nGalileo Galilei (1564–1642) used the telescope to observe mountains on the moon, moons of Jupiter, and sunspots—evidence inconsistent with Aristotelian cosmology. His experimental approach—dropping balls from the Tower of Pisa to measure acceleration—established the empirical method. His conflict with the Catholic Inquisition became the iconic case of science vs. religious authority.\n\nIsaac Newton (1643–1727) synthesized the revolution in his Principia Mathematica (1687). His law of universal gravitation and three laws of motion provided a single mathematical framework explaining both celestial (planetary orbits) and terrestrial (falling objects) mechanics. Newton\'s synthesis was the crowning achievement of the Scientific Revolution.\n\nThe Scientific Revolution introduced the scientific method: systematic observation, hypothesis formation, experimental testing, and mathematical description—an approach to knowledge fundamentally different from Aristotelian deduction or biblical authority. It provided the intellectual foundation for the Enlightenment and the Industrial Revolution.',
